{{also|Floss|FLOSS}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|flɒs|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-floss.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒs|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
{{wikipedia|Dental floss}}
[[Image:Spinning candy floss.jpg|thumb|right|A woman spinning candy floss.]]

1750, from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|floche||tuft of wool|lang=fr}}, from {{term|floc|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|flosche||down, velvet|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|floccus||piece of wool|lang=la}}, probably from {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*flokko||down, wool, flock}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*flukkōn-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*flukkan-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fluksōn-||down, flock}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*plAwək-||hair, fibres, tuft}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|flocko||down|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|vlocke||flock|lang=dum}}, {{etyl|no|-}} dialectal {{term|flugsa||snowflake|lang=no}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|flos||plush|tr=17c.|lang=nl}}. Related to [[fleece]].<!-- ?? -->

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# a [[thread]], used to clean the area between the [[teeth]]
# (raw) [[silk]] fibres
# the fibres covering a [[corn]] [[cob]]
# Any thread-like material having parallel [[strands]] that are not [[spun]] or [[wound]] around each other.
#: ''embroidery '''floss'''''
# {{context|British|lang=en}} Spun sugar or [[cotton candy]], especially in the phrase "candy floss".

